00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.740
So when our clients start to
talk about moving to cloud,

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.490
there's this temptation that we
see of clients looking to say,

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.890
there's all kinds of technical
problems, technical debt,

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.450
and issues that I need
to resolve before I move.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.150
And when I'm move
to cloud, I want

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.220
to take advantage,
full advantage

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.680
of all of the
capabilities and features

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.450
that cloud has to offer.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.160
And what we see is that
can lead to problems.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.010
So as an example,
one of my clients,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:31.790
which is an oil and
gas conglomerate,

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.310
basically, they looked at their
environment, and they said,

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.470
we need to fix all of our
Active Directory issues.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.680
We need to fix several
security issues.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.510
And we want to take
all of our applications

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.156
to PaaS, cloudify everything.

00:02:44.156 --> 00:02:45.530
And about two,
two and half years

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.750
later, they basically have
nothing to show for it,

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.600
because they're just stuck.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:52.410
So in that case,
greed is not good.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.360
When we start to
think about planning,

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.010
We have to look at the ideal
situation and the situation

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.210
in the real world.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.480
And in the ideal situation,
every application

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.921
would be fully standalone.

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.920
The application stack
would be fully standalone.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.200
So you have an isolated
application server, web server,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.900
database server, I
mean, no interaction.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.160
And then even if you
peel away the technology,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.395
the user communities would
be completely separate,

00:03:20.395 --> 00:03:21.850
there'd be no interaction.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:24.990
You had no interaction
or no conflicts

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.080
with other business priorities
or other initiatives.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.060
But we all know
that's not the case.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.540
So the reality of the
situation is everything

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.440
that we all work on is
very much intertwined.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:38.010
You have shared
database servers.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.540
You have shared web farms.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.360
You have application
teams that are

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.450
focused on several priorities
across different applications.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.250
And you have to work
around those things.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.420
Those are all
things that we have

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.790
to address when we're thinking
about migration planning.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.320
So if we talk about
planning, it's

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.250
really not a binary situation.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.770
It really is a spectrum.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.680
And on the far left-hand
bookend of that,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.920
you really have this concept of
just throw stuff on the truck

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.370
and move it.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.990
And that's really--
you walk in the door,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.875
you grab a few things, or you
grab your entire portfolio

00:04:14.875 --> 00:04:18.190
of applications and
servers, and you move it,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.670
and then you just
see what happens.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.961
And what we see-- we actually
do have some clients that

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.470
try to do this on their own.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.120
And what we see
that happens is they

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.890
don't understand the impacts.

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.130
They experience major
issues during the migration,

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:34.420
problems with latency, problems
with application and GUI

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:35.890
performance.

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.844
And ultimately, their
migration loses momentum,

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:41.260
because they lose the support
of the business and the user

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:42.790
community.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:44.830
On the other end of
the spectrum is really

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:46.150
the scientific approach.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.060
And it's this concept of
must understand everything

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:52.215
about the applications and the
servers before you move them.

00:04:52.215 --> 00:04:53.590
And quite frankly,
that's just as

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:56.230
bad as the concept of throw
everything on the truck,

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:59.410
because you can really get stuck
in that analysis-paralysis mode

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.900
and not want to move until
you have perfect information.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.489
And so the goal is to find
somewhere in between that suits

00:05:05.489 --> 00:05:07.405
your organization and
your migration approach.

00:05:12.707 --> 00:05:14.790
One of the next things
that we need to talk about,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.380
as we look at
migration planning,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.710
is what are your business
drivers for migrating to cloud?

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.170
When we first started migrating
to cloud a few years ago,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.610
really the drivers
were all around

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:26.107
these concepts of cost
savings and efficiency,

00:05:26.107 --> 00:05:27.690
get things into the
cloud where we can

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.370
size for average utilization.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.160
We don't have to size for peak.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.750
Start to make use of that
as-a-service capability,

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.890
turn things off
and on as needed.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.940
But now, as we look
into cloud, there's

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.160
many more business
drivers and values

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:43.439
that you can get out
of that, the IT agility

00:05:43.439 --> 00:05:45.480
and the innovation, and,
ultimately, the business

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.110
growth and business agility.

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:52.020
So we're moving beyond IT
cost to IT generating revenue,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.727
to really cloud-driving
business revenue.

00:05:54.727 --> 00:05:56.310
And you have to take
that into account

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.100
as you do your planning.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.190
And one example to
think about that

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.500
is a retail client that we had.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.540
Originally, they had started
to look at cost savings.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:08.910
That was their primary driver.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.620
But when they got
into it, they realized

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.590
they could start to take
advantage of the analytics

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.470
and machine learning
capabilities

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:18.390
and really immediately jump to
getting this business growth

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.675
and business agility.

00:06:19.675 --> 00:06:21.300
So again, those are
some of the factors

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.300
you need to think about.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.430
So you'll hear us talk
about the different steps

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.590
and the different
data points that we

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.590
think you need to collect when
you doing migration planning.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.650
And planning, really,
to a lot of people,

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:41.800
is all about, what date are
we going to migrate things?

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.907
And we would argue that you also
need to think about how are you

00:06:45.907 --> 00:06:46.740
going to migrate it?

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.990
What approach are
you going to take?

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.960
And really don't think
about that as one approach

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.220
for your entire migration.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.700
Think about that on a
per application basis.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:59.670
So there's really
a ladder or a curve

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.450
here of effort versus benefit.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:06.060
And the first adoption
or migration method

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.300
is really this concept of
data center consolidation

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.700
or basic capabilities.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.990
And this would be moving
applications and servers

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:19.290
primarily to consolidate
your data centers.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:24.300
You'll hear people moving to
cloud just the DR environment.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.030
Or you may hear them move
content to part of the content

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.860
delivery network in
order to take off

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:34.170
some of the capacity out of
their existing structure.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.580
The next step up in
that is to just do

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.320
IaaS lift-and-shift migration.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.350
So just take your
existing applications,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.230
with minimal configuration
changes and adjustments,

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:47.416
and move them into cloud as is.

00:07:47.416 --> 00:07:48.790
And then you
continue to ramp up,

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.070
and you get into a little
bit of transformation,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:54.350
moving to containers and
other technologies like that.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.190
Or you can do full-blown
transformation

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.120
and really recode or
refactor your application

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.850
and take full advantage
of all of the features

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.760
and capabilities that
cloud has to offer.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:18.960
And like I said, one of the
things to think about here

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.960
is that each application, you
can really pick and choose

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:22.740
here.

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.360
If you have an application
that is not maintained but is

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.490
heavily used for certain
periods of the month,

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.920
that really makes sense from
a lift-and-shift perspective.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.600
You're not going to
get a lot of benefit

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.049
out of refactoring and
recoding that application.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.070
Whereas, on the far other
end of the spectrum,

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:40.080
if you have an
application that's

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.510
driving a lot of business,
there's a lot of data coming,

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.901
going, it's very actively
developed, that's

00:08:44.901 --> 00:08:47.400
going to make a lot of sense
to think about a transformation

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:48.816
approach and go
through the effort

00:08:48.816 --> 00:08:50.655
and work to transform
that application

00:08:50.655 --> 00:08:59.630
and recode it from
scratch, potentially

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:03.020
So as we start to talk
about migration methodology,

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:04.820
there's really four
challenges, that we're

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.340
trying to overcome in
the migration, when

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:08.930
we think about planning.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:11.520
The first is understanding
the inventory.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.640
A lot of people
struggle to understand,

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:16.270
what are all of the servers
they have in their environment?

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:18.020
What are all of the
applications that they

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:19.790
have in their environment?

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:23.920
And then that really leads us
into the next step, which is--

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.120
sorry, the next challenge,
which is understanding

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:27.950
the dependencies between those.

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:29.840
Which applications
use which servers?

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.480
Which ones are divided out into
your different environments

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.520
between your non-production and
your production environments?

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:36.980
And then probably
more importantly,

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:40.010
for migration and dealing with
some of the additional network

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.950
latency that we take on
when we move to cloud,

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.360
is understanding these
application-to-application

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.900
interfaces.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.510
So when you move
one application,

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.390
what's the impact going to be
to the end user experience?

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.150
And is the application still
going to be functional?

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:54.770
And then, on the
back end, what's

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:58.680
the impact of batching
those kind of things?

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.730
The third challenge there
is around optimizing

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.320
these repeated efforts.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:07.400
If you're moving more than
a few hundred servers,

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.530
there's a script or
a process that you're

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:10.779
going to want to follow.

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:12.320
And that's one of
the reasons we talk

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.020
about these different
migration patterns.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.101
And you want to be as efficient
in that process as possible

00:10:19.101 --> 00:10:20.600
and have the same
script that you're

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.160
executing, over and over.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:24.680
You don't want every
migration to be

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.491
a snowflake that you have to
figure things out every time.

00:10:28.491 --> 00:10:30.740
And the last piece that
we're-- or last challenge that

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:32.780
we're trying to address
in planning is really

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.570
understanding your
resourcing needs.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:38.780
Every organization that I know
of is resource-constrained.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.090
And you have to figure out
how to work through that.

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:42.860
And how you do that is--

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:46.075
one of the ways that you address
that is with proper planning

00:10:46.075 --> 00:10:48.200
and laying that out, laying
out your different move

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:49.910
types across your
schedule, so that you

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.410
can predict what your resource
requirements are going to be.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:03.310
One of the reasons that we like
our methodology for planning

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.050
these migrations
is that we really

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:08.300
take a business-driven
approach to this.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:10.420
So you'll hear of
some migrations

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:11.685
being very server-centric.

00:11:11.685 --> 00:11:13.810
They talk in, and they'll
just pick up some servers

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:14.980
and move them.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:17.980
And I would argue that's
a recipe for disaster.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.560
That's going to cause
problems, straightaway.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.530
If you walk in, and you begin
working with the business

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:27.160
and then go downward,
top down, you

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.720
should see a lot
more success when

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:30.100
you're doing your planning.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.860
So you start by talking
with the business,

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:35.380
understanding which business
units want to migrate,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:38.140
make sure they understand
the value of that.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.640
And then naturally,
you should start

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:44.200
to see business units that are
early adopters or groups even

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.930
within business units
that are early adopters,

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:52.150
and some business units that
want to migrate later or last.

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.670
From there, then you
can start to dive

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:57.630
into their applications,
the different environments,

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:01.893
get to your servers and
your shared infrastructure.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:14.930
So this is really the guts of
our migration planning process.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:19.900
And-- whoops-- there we go.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:22.460
So it starts on
the left-hand side,

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.692
and that's with understanding
your application inventory.

00:12:25.692 --> 00:12:27.400
And I think it's
probably well understood

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.250
that you need to understand
what all the applications are

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.000
in your environment,
and you need to

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:36.350
from a technology perspective.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.410
But you'll also
benefit if you'll

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:41.200
start to understand
also the business

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:42.850
view of the application.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:43.870
When is it used?

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:44.710
How is it used?

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.810
How many users?

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.150
Components like security.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.280
If you do have a little
bit of technical debt

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.200
that you want to
address, understanding

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:56.120
those kind of components.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.060
And then the same situation
on the infrastructure side.

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:07.740
What are all of those
servers in your environment?

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:10.710
What operating system
are they running?

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.310
What operating
system-- and that's

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:15.840
particularly if the OS is not
supported in your future state

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.710
environment, or you want to
use the opportunity to upgrade,

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:21.272
but collecting that
kind of information.

00:13:21.272 --> 00:13:22.980
From there, then you're
starting to build

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:25.680
that mapping or those
linkages between the servers

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.960
and the applications.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:33.180
And again, that's both
the application-to-server,

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.290
which is one part of it.

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.789
But probably the
more important part

00:13:35.789 --> 00:13:37.584
of that and the
more difficult part

00:13:37.584 --> 00:13:39.000
is that
application-to-application

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:39.690
mapping.

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:41.500
You have to understand
those interfaces

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:44.190
so that when you pick
them up and move them,

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:47.070
you move the highly-coupled
applications together,

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:52.430
at the same time, and maintain
that end user usability.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:55.070
So then once you map
those infrastructure

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.910
to applications and
applications to applications,

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:01.790
then you need to go through the
process of identifying the move

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:02.960
approach.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:07.100
And it's back to determining
which of those applications

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:11.100
are going to get the value
of cloud and to what extent.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:16.340
So if you want to assign
the IaaS lift-and-shift,

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:18.760
if you want to do--

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:21.640
or refactoring, or
containerization,

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:24.320
you need to do all of
that thinking, or at least

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:27.300
an initial pass, during the
migration planning phase.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:34.150
Then, once you have that
mapping and that move approach,

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:38.980
you take that mapping and you
use that to create your bundles

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:40.120
or move groups.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.270
And in our
methodology, the bundle

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.700
is really the atomic
unit or the smallest unit

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:50.150
of applications and servers
that can move at the same time.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:51.620
So you know that,
as you're talking

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:54.919
about doing a move, that
bundle, that group of servers

00:14:54.919 --> 00:14:56.710
and applications, all
has to move together.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:59.840
And if there's a problem, it
all will move back together.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.580
And likewise, when you start
to think about waves, to us,

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.500
a wave is just a date.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:10.020
So a wave is a collection
of bundles on a date.

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:15.840
So then once you have that
bundle, or those atomic units

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:18.720
of servers and applications that
need to move at the same time

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.970
to preserve that
end user experience,

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.280
then you can put
them on the schedule.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.310
And that schedule really
starts with a straw man

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:29.970
or a framework of what are
your possible move dates.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.820
And typically, we see
certain dates fall out

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:35.070
because of month
end fiscal close,

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:37.170
freeze periods having to
do with either busiest

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:40.710
times of the year or
with holiday seasons.

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:43.650
You could also start to see
components of the schedule

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:46.800
fall out for particular business
units or particular application

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:49.230
teams because of other
campaigns or other initiatives

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:50.820
they have going on.

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:54.000
But that's where you place those
bundles on those move dates.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.910
And then once you have that, you
have a fairly robust schedule,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:00.157
at that point, that you can
use for resource estimation

00:16:00.157 --> 00:16:01.240
and those kinds of things.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:07.750
So how we handle this from
an Accenture standpoint

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:11.710
is we actually have built,
instantiated our methodology

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.710
into a tool called
Accelerate for Cloud.

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:18.700
And Accelerate for Cloud
really is a workflow tool.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:21.520
When we looked at the
tooling environment-- so

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.230
there's a lot of
great tools out there

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:24.640
to drive these migrations.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.052
But they're technical tools.

00:16:26.052 --> 00:16:27.760
They're good tools
for taking the servers

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:31.010
and the applications and
getting them up into the cloud.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.920
There's not really
a lot of good tools

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:36.220
to drive the work
behind the migration

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:38.182
and drive all the tasks.

00:16:38.182 --> 00:16:40.390
And so we have this tool
called Accelerate for Cloud.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.660
And it takes us all the way
from that inventory stage,

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.160
where you load in the inventory,
and then you start to match

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:46.060
and map it.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:48.340
It takes us through an
application questionnaire

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.830
process, where we ask the--

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.760
we load it up with
information from a CMDB,

00:16:54.760 --> 00:16:56.980
other spreadsheets, and
any other data sources that

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:59.680
are possible, and then that gets
passed back to the application

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.790
owners to validate
that information

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:06.160
and plug-in other things that
we need for the migration.

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:09.250
And then, from there, we can
use that to drive the schedule.

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:10.634
We use it to check our bundles.

00:17:10.634 --> 00:17:12.550
So it actually keeps
track of all the mappings

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:14.290
that we've identified
and the interfaces

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.837
between applications.

00:17:15.837 --> 00:17:17.920
And then we can use that
to validate and make sure

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.950
that we've accommodated all
those interfaces as we're

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.020
putting the bundles together.

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:25.060
And then it turns out
a number of reports

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:28.270
that allows us to take reports
back to the business and say,

00:17:28.270 --> 00:17:30.770
here's when all of your
applications are migrating.

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:33.400
Go to individual owners and
say, here's the different events

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:34.510
that you'll have.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:38.440
Is that good or bad based on
your scenario and situation?

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.120
And then, ultimately,
it drives through

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.970
to the final migration,
which is beyond planning.

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:45.760
But it gets us down into
15-minute increments

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:47.230
during the actual
migration where

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:50.660
we can track the migration of
hundreds of servers at a time.

00:17:56.138 --> 00:17:57.545
MAMOUN HIRZALLA: Thanks Greg.

00:17:57.545 --> 00:17:58.628
GREG BUXKEMPER: Thank you.

00:18:01.877 --> 00:18:03.460
MAMOUN HIRZALLA: Can
you hear me guys?

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:05.040
OK, great.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:08.390
So that was an important part
of the migration that usually

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:13.220
gets forgotten in many small and
medium type of companies, who

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:18.200
may think that just because I
can take an image or a server

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:22.040
and move it to Google Cloud
Platform, that basically means

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:23.660
the majority of my job is done.

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:26.030
And reality is far,
far, far from that.

00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:29.060
And I'm going to start looking
at some of the more details.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.810
So Greg gave you the
20,000-foot view.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:35.300
I'm going now down to about the
1,000-foot view to talk about

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:37.250
how we can do some
of this in GCP,

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:40.010
and what are the implications,
and the things that you need

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:42.650
to be aware of, and what type
of some of the adoption patterns

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:45.360
that we see as we talk to
our customers in the field.

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:52.130
So, there are customers
who would basically

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:56.780
still insist on the fact that we
still want to do it on our own.

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:59.720
And for those who are insisting
to do it on their own,

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:02.240
basically all the things
that Greg talked about,

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:04.550
whether that's the
planning, figuring out

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:08.810
the inventory, the mapping, the
bundling, the scheduling, all

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:12.410
of that, that basically
goes back to your lap.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.884
And if you were to look at
these type of activities

00:19:14.884 --> 00:19:17.300
here, whether it's assessment,
planning, or the networking

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:20.282
scheduling, or the
application, or whatever

00:19:20.282 --> 00:19:21.740
additional activities
that you need

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.755
to do, you need to take a deep
look, within your organization,

00:19:24.755 --> 00:19:27.560
and say, do I have the
capability, the skills,

00:19:27.560 --> 00:19:30.770
the time, and willing
to assume the risk

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:32.960
if I were to do that on my own?

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:34.340
So that's a key
question you need

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:36.890
to think about as
part of your taking

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:39.830
that upon yourself to go
and do it from A to Z. So

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:42.830
that's just something to point
out, because there are still

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:45.800
these folks out in the field
that they think they can do it

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:48.200
from A to Z on their
own, without looking

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:50.300
at it from a holistic
type of perspectives

00:19:50.300 --> 00:19:54.020
and understanding what are
the implications of the app

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:56.490
migration type of process.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.640
Now, if you were to look
at where Google can help.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.240
I'm sure many of you have looked
at the Google Cloud Platform,

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.790
and that's why you're here
trying to look at, well,

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:07.700
what are the tools, the things
that would help me as part

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:09.111
of the migration process.

00:20:09.111 --> 00:20:10.610
So if you were to
look at this slide

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:13.550
basically it represents
different consumption models

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:16.760
and the things that are the
typical suspects for migrating

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:17.772
to the cloud.

00:20:17.772 --> 00:20:19.730
So when you look at
Infrastructure-as-a-Service

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:23.370
platform, as a service in SaaS,
different companies would be

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:24.980
at different end
of the spectrum.

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.650
And some companies may be
at all ends of the spectrum

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.080
when it comes to these
type of consumption models.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.780
So from Google's perspective,
we have different type

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:35.930
of constructs or
capabilities that

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:38.679
would allow you to say,
OK, well in terms of SaaS,

00:20:38.679 --> 00:20:40.220
one of the key things
that we look at

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:41.810
is Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.780
And that's one that we
want to basically just try

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:46.400
to get our data
on it, start doing

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:48.590
some backups, disaster
recovery, and all

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:50.030
of these type of things.

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:52.160
PaaS perspective, we
have lots of constructs

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.560
that would help you to
get there as well in terms

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:59.090
of the data that would be
required to be migrated

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:00.290
with these type of apps.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:02.180
And these are some
of the constructs,

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:05.716
whether it's CloudSQL,
Datastore BigTable, BigQuery,

00:21:05.716 --> 00:21:06.840
Spanner, it doesn't matter.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:08.506
All of these things
are managed for you.

00:21:08.506 --> 00:21:11.850
And they can be used as
part of the migration.

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:14.450
And if you're looking
to import or export data

00:21:14.450 --> 00:21:16.280
from SaaS type of
applications, you

00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:19.310
can leverage the
services of Dataflow that

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:20.840
would allow you to do that.

00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:22.550
From application
perspective, you

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.540
have different set
of options, as well.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:27.470
So if you're starting
with IaaS, you

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.630
have compute type
of capabilities

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:33.350
with GCE, Google Compute
Engine type of capabilities.

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:36.350
And you have also the ability
to leverage containers

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.040
as part of the process.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:41.310
And then you also have,
from PaaS perspective,

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:43.700
if you don't want to go
and fiddle with that,

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.480
you can look at it
and say, OK, well,

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:48.530
I want to have the
ability to just not worry

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.380
about that and leverage
things that are already

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:52.430
procured for me.

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:55.100
And that's where you
leverage Google App Engine.

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:58.190
And Kubernetes comes
somewhere in between the two.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:00.110
And certainly, Apigee
comes into the picture,

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:02.810
because, if you want
to monetize or provide

00:22:02.810 --> 00:22:06.200
these type of API interfaces
to your applications,

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:09.640
that's where Apigee can
be part of the process.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.790
Now, what are the migrations
that we see in the field

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:14.050
when it comes to GCP?

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:15.800
Let's start with a simple case.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:18.470
The simplest of cases
is Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.080
So this is basically
somebody that's

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:22.210
trying to do a backup
disaster recovery.

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:25.150
Or somebody may have
a static website

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:28.330
that only contains,
in this point, just

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:32.050
HTML and some JavaScript that
basically what you can do,

00:22:32.050 --> 00:22:35.080
you can just migrate all your
content into Google Cloud

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:35.660
Storage.

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:36.240
It's global.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.080
It's replicated.

00:22:38.080 --> 00:22:40.420
Content is accessible
through URI.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:41.950
You can point a DNS to it.

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:44.630
The whole website can be
up and running in minutes.

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:46.420
So that's something
that you can do.

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:49.900
If you want to leverage CDNs,
you can also look at the ones

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:51.490
that Google has
partnered with that

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.710
makes it even more faster
type of access to it

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:56.410
and caching at the
edge of the network.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:58.720
If you are saying, well, OK,
that's too simple for me.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:22:59.980
I have more complex stuff.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.840
And that's where you can start
looking at the Google Compute

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:03.340
Engine.

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:06.730
And we start looking at, well,
what type of raw capabilities

00:23:06.730 --> 00:23:10.240
and power that Google Cloud
Platform brings to the table?

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:11.890
So that's where
you start looking

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:15.460
at different type of machines
that are available to you,

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.360
whether it's Linux, Windows,
and live migration from Google

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:20.470
as part of the process.

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:23.119
Or you can look
at it from I have

00:23:23.119 --> 00:23:24.910
standard machines that
are available to you

00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:26.240
out of the box.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:28.090
I need something that's
completely different

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:29.260
because I'm special.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:31.000
You can still go
and do that, create

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.520
the amount of CPUs
and RAM for you

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:35.530
and the storage that
caters to your needs

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:39.080
rather than using what
Google offers out of the box.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:42.250
You can have the ability
to create custom images.

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:43.390
And you can take snapshots.

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:45.640
You can make these snapshots
available to other people

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.420
in different projects.

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:53.010
You can also create or deploy a
container image as part of GCE.

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:54.620
So if you have built
your containers,

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:58.150
you can deploy them
directly on VMs from Google

00:23:58.150 --> 00:23:59.660
as part of the process.

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:04.450
And you have the ability
to procure up to 160 cores.

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:06.580
I just checked
that in the console

00:24:06.580 --> 00:24:09.200
to see what's the maximum,
because that number changes.

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:12.340
So you have up to 160 cores,
four terabytes of RAM,

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:16.150
and up to 64
terabytes of storage

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:17.650
that are available to you.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:20.140
And on top of all of that,
you get all the benefits

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:23.650
of elastic type of capabilities
autoscale up, autoscale down,

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:25.570
monitoring, all of
these things that you

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:28.480
get as part of Google
Cloud Platform.

00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:31.190
On the other end
of the spectrum,

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:35.470
you might come and say, you know
what, I don't have the patience

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:36.820
to deal with all of this.

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:39.220
I want something
that's much easier.

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:42.292
I just want my developer's to
go give me a Java environment,

00:24:42.292 --> 00:24:43.750
and they just want
to start coding.

00:24:43.750 --> 00:24:46.540
I don't want to fiddle with
IaaS and do any of this machine

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:47.650
setup or any of that.

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:48.280
Great.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:50.410
So that's where
Google App Engine

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:52.890
comes into the picture
as part of the process.

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:54.640
You have two flavors
of Google App Engine.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:56.390
You have the Flex and Standard.

00:24:56.390 --> 00:24:58.180
Standard, pretty much the same.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:01.240
They're both fully-managed
type of environments.

00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:03.730
They're autoscaling their
version as part of the process.

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:07.390
Flex gives you more capabilities
than what Standard offers.

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:09.280
Standard offers four languages.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:12.640
Flex offers you four
plus two and any type

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:14.590
of language environment
that you want to add,

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:17.590
because you can incorporate
those as part of your process.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:19.876
You have the ability to do the--

00:25:19.876 --> 00:25:21.250
all of these type
of environments

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:24.400
are backed up for you
using the Datastore, SQL,

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:27.870
or GCS Cloud Storage that
are available out of the box.

00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:30.440
They autoscale to zero
for the standard part.

00:25:30.440 --> 00:25:32.080
So basically you
can use it for free.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:34.390
If you build it up,
and nobody's using it,

00:25:34.390 --> 00:25:36.070
you don't have to pay for it.

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:38.370
For the Flex, it
scales down to one.

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.650
So somebody would have to pay
for that if nobody's using it.

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:43.930
And Flex also gives you
additional capabilities

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:46.300
like root access if you
want to, where you don't

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:48.760
have that as part of standard.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:51.320
And if you're coming and
saying, well, that's great,

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:55.630
but I'm a company who is running
significant type of workloads

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:58.140
that are running on my
on-prem, and they're

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:01.420
taking a significant amount
of time to be processed.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:02.740
Can Google help me with that?

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:03.880
Of course they can.

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:07.630
And that's where we start doing
the burst out type of mode

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:09.100
and hybrid type of
clouds where you

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:12.882
can provide some connectivity
between you and Google.

00:26:12.882 --> 00:26:13.840
There are many options.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.600
You can do VPN, direct,
interconnect, peering.

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:19.480
And you can use the
Google Cloud power

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:24.290
to get some of this type of
workloads move to the cloud,

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:25.150
let them run.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:26.800
When you're done,
they scale down,

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:30.010
and you don't pay for additional
work that you don't have to.

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:32.620
And you don't have to procure
any of the infrastructure.

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:36.430
Or you're on-prem, and you
don't have to worry about that.

00:26:36.430 --> 00:26:38.320
OK, now I procured all
this infrastructure,

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:39.290
nobody's using it.

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:41.770
So that's a good option
for those type of companies

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:43.730
who may be interested
in that entry point

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:45.520
as part of Google
Cloud Platform.

00:26:45.520 --> 00:26:47.710
So there are many
options for you.

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:50.170
Look at your potential
use cases, what

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:52.030
you want to do out
of that, and select

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:53.510
the right pattern for you.

00:26:53.510 --> 00:26:57.680
And look at it from a
holistic perspective, as well.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.274
And you can look at it from
a tactical perspective,

00:27:00.274 --> 00:27:01.690
if that is the
only thing that you

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:04.450
want to look at, and
continue with that.

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:10.700
Now, as we look at GCP migration
and type of objectives,

00:27:10.700 --> 00:27:13.640
there are many stations
along the way where

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:16.130
customers look at it
and say, well, OK,

00:27:16.130 --> 00:27:18.530
my objective, from
moving to the cloud,

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:22.400
is really about I want to
have my dev environment

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:25.970
or my whole infrastructure
on Google Cloud,

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:28.800
because I think it's the
right platform for me from dev

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.120
testing and production.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:32.455
That would be one option for
customers looking into it.

00:27:32.455 --> 00:27:33.830
Other customers
may come and say,

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:35.330
you know what, I
just want to use it

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:37.000
for disaster recovery backup.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.680
Or others saying we heard
that you have great tools

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:42.620
and capabilities around big
data and machine learning,

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:44.750
and that becomes the
primary objective

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:46.130
of moving to the cloud.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:50.120
So it really all depends on you,
what you want to do with it.

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:52.220
But for this particular
example, that I'm

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:55.670
trying to explain
here, this is a company

00:27:55.670 --> 00:27:57.970
whose primary objective
was to accelerate

00:27:57.970 --> 00:28:02.000
app delivery and leverage Google
Cloud Platform to do that.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:05.420
And what type of capabilities
that Google offers to you

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:06.860
in this type of realm?

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:11.810
So I talked about the GCE type
of components and the fact

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:14.540
that we have GKE as well
in terms of Kubernetes,

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:17.030
and the fact that we have
these wonderful clusters

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:20.600
that Kubernetes can allow you
to spin and scale up and scale

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:21.934
down automatically.

00:28:21.934 --> 00:28:24.100
You have also the ability
for the Container Builder.

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:26.540
So if you want to build
containers on GCP,

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:28.770
you have the ability to do that.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.620
And if you don't want
to use Docker Hub,

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:32.750
you have an alternative
that is provided

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:35.430
to you through the Container
Registry on Google Cloud

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:36.530
Platform.

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:38.690
You have the ability
to leverage App Engine

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:39.800
as part of the process.

00:28:39.800 --> 00:28:42.350
And just announced,
the Cloud Build

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:45.170
is also one of these tools
that you can use and look

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:48.320
into as part of the arsenal
for building or accelerating

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:50.430
your app delivery on the cloud.

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:54.440
You can also have the ability
to import VMs, take snapshots,

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.450
images, and do anything
you want to with that,

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:59.810
plus the availability of all
these container-optimized

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:01.790
operating systems that
are available to you

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:05.356
out of the box from Google.

00:29:05.356 --> 00:29:06.980
Other customers who
may come look at it

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:09.560
and say, OK, well my destination
is really machine learning

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:13.040
and AI, because there
are many things that

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:16.585
would be beneficial to me
from my particular use case.

00:29:16.585 --> 00:29:17.960
And with machine
learning and AI,

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:20.450
I think one of the key things
just to remember and keep

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.570
in mind is to sort
the hype from facts.

00:29:23.570 --> 00:29:26.280
And the first thing
you need to do,

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:29.330
whether it's Google Cloud
Platform or any other type

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.529
of cloud provider,
is to understand

00:29:31.529 --> 00:29:34.070
what are the real use cases that
you're trying to accomplish.

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:36.020
Lots of companies
right now are saying

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:38.145
we want to do machine
learning and AI without fully

00:29:38.145 --> 00:29:39.590
understanding what
does that mean

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:42.950
or without fully selecting
the right use cases

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:44.720
and having the
right data in order

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:46.400
to get to machine learning.

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:49.310
So once you put that aside,
you have the right data,

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:52.220
you have the right use cases,
you've done your homework, now

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:54.960
what Google Cloud Platform
can help me as part of that--

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.750
and we have tons
of capabilities--

00:29:56.750 --> 00:29:58.880
but it all starts
with the basics.

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:01.760
The basic is basically getting
your data to Google Cloud.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:05.090
And we have lots of tools,
from Google Perspective,

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:08.150
that allows me to cater to a
structured, non-structured type

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:12.680
of data, allow me to cater to
LTP or all app type of loads.

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:16.700
So it really depends on you,
what is the right platform

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:20.030
or construct for you to get
the data to Google Cloud.

00:30:20.030 --> 00:30:21.740
Once you make these
type of decisions,

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:23.450
you start looking at
what type of tools

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:25.480
that are available
to me in terms of,

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:27.410
I want to use the
Google Transfer Service,

00:30:27.410 --> 00:30:29.330
or I want to use
gsutil, or I want

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.490
to use any of these
providers that

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.440
offer the transfer
type of capabilities

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:35.520
to me as part of the process.

00:30:35.520 --> 00:30:37.100
So there are many
options to you.

00:30:37.100 --> 00:30:40.280
And in previous session, I
talked about these options

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.530
in detail, and data
migration to cloud.

00:30:42.530 --> 00:30:45.800
It's a whole session about that,
so can not cover all of it.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.870
But these are part
of the key decisions

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:49.690
that you need to
make as you start

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:52.310
in this particular journey
to machine learning.

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:53.990
And then you also
have the ability

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:56.390
to do the cloud native
support for Hadoop.

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.280
And that's in the
form of Dataproc.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.440
And they just announced
the autoscaling

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:03.680
of Dataproc clusters, which
is a great thing to do.

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:07.670
It's in beta right now,
but it soon will be in GA.

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:09.870
And that would allow you
to automatically scale up,

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:11.930
scale down in these
type of clusters

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:15.140
as you move your Hadoop
workloads into Google Cloud

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.130
Platform.

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.410
And then we have the
best-of-breed analytics

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:19.770
and machine learning.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:25.250
TensorFlow is the number
one adopted open source

00:31:25.250 --> 00:31:26.630
tool for machine learning.

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:29.030
It's open source by
Google, so it has been

00:31:29.030 --> 00:31:30.380
used in Google for a long time.

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.040
It's the number one adopted.

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:35.210
And we have the pre-built
models, the perception APIs,

00:31:35.210 --> 00:31:39.440
and AutoML type of capabilities
that are available to you.

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:42.770
Plus from exploration
and analysis,

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:46.040
you have the Data Studio, the
Datalabs, and Google Analytics

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:48.890
360 that are part of
the mix of the tools

00:31:48.890 --> 00:31:50.870
that you need to look
into as you migrate

00:31:50.870 --> 00:31:54.560
or your destination becomes
the machine learning.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:56.420
Now, a question that
always comes up,

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:59.980
as I move to the cloud,
lots of companies

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:02.920
or lots of individuals
think that re-architecting

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:05.090
is one of those
sexy things that I

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:07.820
have to have when I
migrate to the cloud.

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:09.680
And the answer is
not necessarily true,

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:13.670
because there are lots of
statistics that show that there

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:15.330
is value in lift-and-shift.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:19.700
So I really don't have to just
re-architect my applications

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:22.190
to the cloud to gain the
benefits of the cloud.

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:25.160
There are cases where
it makes a lot of sense

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:28.730
to go and re-architect the
whole application or portions

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:30.770
of the application
so that you could

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:32.670
leverage these type of things.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.370
But I think one
of the key things

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:37.130
that you need to look at,
am I, as an organization,

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:40.070
equipped with the
right tools and skills

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:42.470
to take on such an endeavor?

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:46.070
So re-architecting and going
into microservice's pattern

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:48.560
and all that stuff, and
the impact on DevOps,

00:32:48.560 --> 00:32:52.700
and how you go from regular
monolith type of systems

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:55.352
into suddenly
microservices, and how

00:32:55.352 --> 00:32:56.810
that impacts the
whole organization

00:32:56.810 --> 00:32:58.010
is not a trivial one.

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.720
So you need to be really aware
of the implications of doing

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:01.220
that.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:04.310
And once you cross that barrier,
then yes, re-architecting

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:07.280
could be a great thing to
do, for the right use cases,

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:08.360
for the right situations.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:10.680
In this type of example
that I show here,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.360
this happens to be
an insurance company.

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:16.080
They had a great algorithm
for underwriting risk.

00:33:16.080 --> 00:33:18.620
However, that algorithm was
not taking into consideration

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:20.150
some of the
additional data that's

00:33:20.150 --> 00:33:21.590
coming from different sources.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.370
And they wanted to
do something with it.

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:25.230
So what to do with that?

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.350
So they said, OK, there
are certain pieces that

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:30.740
can be refactored, where we
can start looking at that data,

00:33:30.740 --> 00:33:34.430
incorporate it, and then
I can create an API so

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:37.530
that other components of the
business could leverage that,

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:39.230
a new type of functionality.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:43.410
And re-architecting made sense
in this particular example.

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:45.740
So it really depends
on your situation.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:49.970
Now a question comes up also
with the GCP migration tools

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:51.290
from Google.

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:53.000
What's out of the box for me?

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.730
If you were to go to
a project in Google,

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:57.530
and you look at
import VM, you will

00:33:57.530 --> 00:34:01.330
be provided with two options,
Cloud Endure and Velostrata.

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:04.660
May 2018, just about
two months ago, Google

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.180
announced that they're
acquiring Velostrata,

00:34:07.180 --> 00:34:08.980
and it would become
a Google company

00:34:08.980 --> 00:34:10.136
in the coming month or so.

00:34:10.136 --> 00:34:11.719
There are many
sessions on Velostrata,

00:34:11.719 --> 00:34:15.250
but the primary differentiator
between the two, Cloud Endure

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:17.330
is a block-level
type of migration.

00:34:17.330 --> 00:34:20.110
So if you have an application
with 10 terabytes, 100

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:22.840
terabytes, it's a
block-level migration.

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:24.909
That means the whole thing
needs to be migrated,

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:27.670
and it cannot be operated
until everything gets migrated

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:28.330
to the cloud.

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:35.860
Velostrata, on the other hand,
allows you to split the storage

00:34:35.860 --> 00:34:37.000
from compute.

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:39.850
And it migrates the
compute to the cloud.

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:42.699
And it allows you to
execute on the compute part

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:44.980
while it reaches out
to the data that's

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:49.010
still in the on-prem
type of situation.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:52.150
And then silently,
it migrates the data,

00:34:52.150 --> 00:34:54.020
streams it into the cloud.

00:34:54.020 --> 00:34:56.170
And if something goes
wrong, you can roll it back,

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:57.430
and it stays where it is.

00:34:57.430 --> 00:35:01.390
So it's a pretty neat type of
concept as part of the process.

00:35:01.390 --> 00:35:05.290
On average, it's about 10
minutes for migrating images.

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:07.630
There is an example, on
YouTube where they migrate

00:35:07.630 --> 00:35:09.760
SugarCRM in about 11 minutes.

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:12.420
And it's pretty impressive.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:15.850
Now, I'll just go
back and remind you

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:17.410
of what Greg mentioned earlier.

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:20.860
Greed is not good, because you
can come and say, OK, well,

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:23.680
if it's that easy, let's
go on start migrating.

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:25.120
No, the answer is not that.

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:28.000
I'm not trying to say that,
yes, it's easy and nice,

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:31.060
but all the things that
Greg have talked about,

00:35:31.060 --> 00:35:33.402
the dependencies, the
infrastructure, all of that,

00:35:33.402 --> 00:35:35.110
and here are things
that you need to look

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:37.540
at as part of your migration.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:40.720
As you start, where
are these VMs going?

00:35:40.720 --> 00:35:42.400
What is my project's structure?

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:46.390
Am I creating one project,
multiple projects, 10 projects?

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:49.360
What am I doing about my network
and VPC and IP management?

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:52.390
Is that happening on its own,
or is somebody doing it for me?

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:54.180
What are my DevOps practices?

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:55.960
How my developers
and engineers are

00:35:55.960 --> 00:35:59.800
going to do when these images
are suddenly backing up?

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:01.690
And how do I get images?

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:03.280
Where do I store them?

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:04.840
All of these things
are important.

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:08.087
Identity and access management,
are you creating the groups?

00:36:08.087 --> 00:36:08.920
What type of groups?

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:10.480
What type of roles?

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:13.012
Moving from on-prem to
a cloud type of model,

00:36:13.012 --> 00:36:14.720
are there any things
that you need to do?

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:15.876
Certainly, yes.

00:36:15.876 --> 00:36:18.250
And then you have to look at
from a security perspective,

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:20.859
firewall roles, routes, et
cetera, what's happening there?

00:36:20.859 --> 00:36:22.150
What ports do you need to open?

00:36:22.150 --> 00:36:24.740
Which applications are talking
through which applications?

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.100
And finally, who is going
to manage all of that?

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:29.705
Are you going to leverage
Stackdriver, in what capacity?

00:36:29.705 --> 00:36:31.330
What are you going
to do with the logs?

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:34.030
What type of dashboard
things, intelligence

00:36:34.030 --> 00:36:36.590
that you are going
to glean out of that?

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:38.090
I'm not trying to
make it very hard.

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:41.470
But I'm just giving you a
dose of reality in terms of,

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:44.500
when you migrate, there
are many considerations

00:36:44.500 --> 00:36:46.390
to consider as part
of the process.

00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:48.760
And all the things that
Greg have talked about,

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:52.210
that's the prep work that needs
to happen before you start

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:54.110
putting and importing that VM.

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:56.050
Certainty you need
to start with a POC.

00:36:56.050 --> 00:36:57.820
Start in baby steps.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:36:59.770
See how the process works.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:02.480
Get some comfort level
as part of the process.

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:06.790
And then you can look at a
much grander type of journey

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:09.610
into migrating
process down the road.

00:37:09.610 --> 00:37:12.059
So with that, I'll hand it
back over to my colleague,

00:37:12.059 --> 00:37:13.850
Greg, who will walk
you through an example.

00:37:13.850 --> 00:37:15.869
Then I'll finish the
last two examples.

00:37:15.869 --> 00:37:16.368
Thank you.

00:37:16.368 --> 00:37:20.040
GREG BUXKEMPER: Thank you.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:23.550
So as we look at some of
these client examples,

00:37:23.550 --> 00:37:25.630
we talked about some of
the questions you'd ask.

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:27.421
And I think the technical
questions are all

00:37:27.421 --> 00:37:28.540
pretty apparent to us.

00:37:28.540 --> 00:37:30.750
But we would also
recommend you think

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:32.850
about a different line
of questioning around

00:37:32.850 --> 00:37:36.210
some business questions and
really some project management

00:37:36.210 --> 00:37:37.980
type questions.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:40.440
And I think you'll see
how these played out

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:42.820
in the client examples that
we're going to go through.

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:45.420
But first and foremost, to
shape your migration plan

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:47.040
and how you approach
planning, you

00:37:47.040 --> 00:37:51.060
should talk to the
IT and business

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:54.210
on what are the primary
reasons for moving to cloud?

00:37:54.210 --> 00:37:55.740
Is it just cost
savings, or are you

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.656
trying to get some of
that additional agility,

00:37:57.656 --> 00:38:00.310
and innovation, and
business growth out of that?

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:02.010
And what are the
expected outcomes?

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:04.886
Again, those questions are going
to drive some overall themes

00:38:04.886 --> 00:38:06.510
and help you understand
if your plan is

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:09.730
going to accomplish those.

00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:12.640
And then, from a project
management perspective,

00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:15.340
you really want to think
about, how fast are

00:38:15.340 --> 00:38:17.590
you wanting to move to cloud?

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:19.480
What does that mean from a pace?

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:22.960
So if you want to be
done in six months

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:25.000
and you have 2,000
servers, can you actually

00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:26.980
do that many
servers every month?

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:29.080
Do you have the
capacity to do that?

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:33.500
Or do you need to look at
changing some factor there?

00:38:33.500 --> 00:38:35.600
What's the budget?

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:37.550
If your plan comes
back and tells you

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:39.200
that it's all
refactoring, and you

00:38:39.200 --> 00:38:40.741
know it's going to
take you thousands

00:38:40.741 --> 00:38:42.380
of hours per
application, you probably

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:43.600
don't have the right plan.

00:38:46.639 --> 00:38:48.180
And then the other--
the last couple,

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:51.730
they're just going
to tie into those.

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:53.730
So the first client
example here is

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:56.940
it's a leading marketer and
distributor of beverages.

00:38:56.940 --> 00:39:00.930
And this is an organization
that had about 1,000 servers.

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:05.250
It was a mix of Windows as
well as some proprietary AIX

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:07.920
and an iSeries that
drove a significant part

00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:10.200
of the business process.

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:12.780
And from a business
perspective, they

00:39:12.780 --> 00:39:15.040
were looking to get away
from their heavy assets.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:16.890
And they were looking
to offload everything,

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:20.280
from their data center
to bottling plants,

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.480
and really shut
down some capability

00:39:22.480 --> 00:39:24.720
within this one
particular business unit.

00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:27.900
And so how that played out
into their migration plan

00:39:27.900 --> 00:39:31.620
is it ended up being basically
a lift-and-shift migration

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:36.000
of about 500 servers, just
a regular IaaS migration.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:38.830
But then they looked at
those proprietary platforms.

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:41.940
And for the AIX, they
did the business case,

00:39:41.940 --> 00:39:44.370
and they said that's
worth transforming.

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:47.220
So they didn't have
to pay AIX and some

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:50.530
of the middleware service
charges going forward.

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:54.150
And so they moved about
100, 150 servers that way.

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:55.870
And then they looked
at the iSeries.

00:39:55.870 --> 00:39:57.450
And we looked at a couple
of different things

00:39:57.450 --> 00:39:58.110
to do with that.

00:39:58.110 --> 00:39:59.640
And ultimately they
said, it's too important.

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.764
And we don't want to invest
a lot of money in this.

00:40:01.764 --> 00:40:06.030
And so they kept that
separate and in a colo.

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:08.087
And then, as you would
expect, in any time

00:40:08.087 --> 00:40:10.170
you're going through a
large transformation, about

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:12.690
200 servers, they just fell
off because of de-commissioning

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:14.615
and not being used anymore.

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:19.680
So while overall
it was a success,

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:21.513
and we did a significant
amount of planning,

00:40:21.513 --> 00:40:23.700
there were some lessons
to be learned here.

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:27.690
And this is the initial
migration plan and then

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:30.900
the actual migration plan as
it went through evolutions.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:33.210
And so you can see
that red dashed line

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:36.510
on the left-hand side, that was
the original migration plan.

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:39.900
And then in
parallel, the company

00:40:39.900 --> 00:40:43.470
was executing an SAP
move to cloud, as well,

00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:45.900
and ran into some
problems there.

00:40:45.900 --> 00:40:48.150
That sucked up
resources that we needed

00:40:48.150 --> 00:40:50.910
to use for the
non-SAP migration.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:52.542
And so that shifted out.

00:40:52.542 --> 00:40:54.000
Now, the interesting
thing here is,

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:57.450
while we did have to replan,
because we had collected

00:40:57.450 --> 00:40:59.022
all of the constraint
information

00:40:59.022 --> 00:41:01.230
and some of the other business
and project management

00:41:01.230 --> 00:41:03.000
information when
we did the plan,

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:05.375
we were able to replan in the
course of just a few days,

00:41:05.375 --> 00:41:07.750
take that back to the business
and application team, say,

00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:09.480
does this work for you?

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:12.690
They signed off, and then
we started to execute again.

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:16.176
And then you'll see that we then
had to replan one more time.

00:41:16.176 --> 00:41:17.550
The other thing
that was going on

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:19.470
is they were shifting
their network management

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:21.266
to another provider.

00:41:21.266 --> 00:41:22.890
And that ended up
causing some problems

00:41:22.890 --> 00:41:24.420
with the network
delays and connections

00:41:24.420 --> 00:41:25.140
and those kind of things.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:27.224
And again, we had to go
back to the drawing board,

00:41:27.224 --> 00:41:29.723
but we were able to do that
very quickly, because we already

00:41:29.723 --> 00:41:31.300
had all the data
collected and were

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:33.330
able to lay that out again.

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:37.710
So one of our arguments
here is, certainly, you

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:40.150
need to do pretty good planning.

00:41:40.150 --> 00:41:43.410
But at a certain
point, you won't

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:45.600
have control over some of
the parallel initiatives.

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:47.160
And you're going
to get influenced.

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:48.720
But if you're
planning data is good,

00:41:48.720 --> 00:41:50.760
if you collected the
right information,

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:52.830
you can adjust and pivot
to that very quickly.

00:41:55.788 --> 00:41:57.920
MAMOUN HIRZALLA: Thank you.

00:41:57.920 --> 00:42:00.800
So let's walk through
our second example.

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:03.860
And that's a financial and
health care services company.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:06.170
And that company,
basically they wanted

00:42:06.170 --> 00:42:11.510
to migrate a certain number of
applications for a conference.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:13.530
And they wanted to do it fast.

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:17.690
So it's right from the get
go, do this quickly right.

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:22.550
And the assumption is we have
done the homework around which

00:42:22.550 --> 00:42:25.220
applications we want to
migrate, because these

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:26.670
are the ones that we needed.

00:42:26.670 --> 00:42:30.410
And the candidates
were provided to us.

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:32.870
As part of this process,
you need to look at it

00:42:32.870 --> 00:42:35.480
and say, OK, well, are these the
right applications before you

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:37.830
start the migration process.

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:40.370
Because, yes, the customer
may come and tell you,

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:42.812
these are the applications
that I want to migrate.

00:42:42.812 --> 00:42:44.270
One of the key
things that you need

00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:46.310
to ask, what are
the dependencies?

00:42:46.310 --> 00:42:48.110
Are these applications
a standalone?

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:49.790
Can they function on their own?

00:42:49.790 --> 00:42:52.520
Are there other dependencies
that you can-- so if I

00:42:52.520 --> 00:42:55.520
go and flip the
switch, from A to Z,

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:58.460
and then these applications,
once they go to the cloud,

00:42:58.460 --> 00:43:00.080
is that OK, or are
there other things

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:01.204
that I need to worry about?

00:43:01.204 --> 00:43:03.352
Turned out that one
of these applications

00:43:03.352 --> 00:43:05.060
was not the right
application to migrate,

00:43:05.060 --> 00:43:06.710
because there were
lots of dependencies

00:43:06.710 --> 00:43:08.610
that they were not aware of.

00:43:08.610 --> 00:43:11.300
So even though the
customer is the one who

00:43:11.300 --> 00:43:13.790
recommended what type of
application to migrate,

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:17.450
they were not aware of 100%
of these type of dependencies.

00:43:17.450 --> 00:43:18.950
And that's very common.

00:43:18.950 --> 00:43:20.960
It's not every company
that know everything.

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:23.750
There are lots of people who
know or write this application

00:43:23.750 --> 00:43:26.870
without even documentations
and doing something

00:43:26.870 --> 00:43:28.190
that you may not be aware of.

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:31.050
So just pay attention to
that as part of the process.

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:32.520
So what we did, we
ended up doing--

00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:35.330
we designed the right
secure architecture for them

00:43:35.330 --> 00:43:36.920
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:39.770
We did our due diligence when it
comes to these of applications,

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:42.500
and we selected the right
metaphors from Google

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:43.400
to migrate them.

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:45.410
And the applications
were migrated.

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:47.270
And the project was a success.

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:50.150
From key lessons
learned perspective

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:54.170
is trying to understand what
type of workloads, in general.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:56.300
So I'm going to talk,
in general here,

00:43:56.300 --> 00:43:58.254
about workload prioritization.

00:43:58.254 --> 00:44:00.170
So some companies may
come and tell you, well,

00:44:00.170 --> 00:44:04.040
what is the criteria for
workload prioritization?

00:44:04.040 --> 00:44:08.050
Some companies may approach
it from TPS per second,

00:44:08.050 --> 00:44:09.050
Transactions Per Second.

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:13.190
So the more TPS per second,
most likely that application

00:44:13.190 --> 00:44:15.080
is more of a mission critical.

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:18.179
And that becomes a less
likelihood candidate

00:44:18.179 --> 00:44:19.470
that you need to migrate first.

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:21.710
So that's one of those
things that you probably

00:44:21.710 --> 00:44:24.450
want to put towards the middle
or the bottom of the list,

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.800
because depending on the machine
criticality, if something

00:44:27.800 --> 00:44:29.840
goes wrong, business
may be disrupted.

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:33.050
So you may want to start with
the ones that are more simple,

00:44:33.050 --> 00:44:36.200
the ones that has less
interfaces, the ones that

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:39.080
may not impact the business
in a significant manner.

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:41.000
Analytical workloads
are typically

00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:44.960
one of these workloads that you
can prioritize to the cloud.

00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:46.550
Because, what you
can do with that,

00:44:46.550 --> 00:44:51.680
you can learn a lot from moving
these types of applications

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:55.520
to the cloud and seeing
what type of lessons learned

00:44:55.520 --> 00:44:58.550
and what type of skills
you have in place,

00:44:58.550 --> 00:45:01.400
so that you can adjust for
the next mission-critical type

00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:04.560
of migrations as
part of the process.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:07.970
The other lessons
learned is skills.

00:45:07.970 --> 00:45:11.060
I think if you are
in the job market

00:45:11.060 --> 00:45:13.460
and looking for somebody
who knows Google Cloud

00:45:13.460 --> 00:45:15.650
Platform very well,
you don't find

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:18.710
many of these people
in the marketplace.

00:45:18.710 --> 00:45:21.560
That is something that a lot
of companies, that we know of,

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:22.560
are struggling.

00:45:22.560 --> 00:45:24.740
And that is something that
you need to pay attention

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:27.080
to as you put your
scheduling plan

00:45:27.080 --> 00:45:28.850
and who is the right
partner that you

00:45:28.850 --> 00:45:33.540
need to work with as part
of the migration process.

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:35.150
The next example
that I'll talk about,

00:45:35.150 --> 00:45:36.240
it's a large global bank.

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:39.870
Basically, that bank
operates in North America

00:45:39.870 --> 00:45:42.720
and has a global presence.

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:44.970
The problem statement
was they wanted

00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:47.250
to migrate a risk
application that

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:49.930
was taking about five
hours to process,

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:55.650
but significant amount of
cost that is being spent

00:45:55.650 --> 00:45:59.400
on the horsepower that is
required to go and figure out

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:02.430
all this type of risk and
all of these calculations

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:03.967
that needed to happen.

00:46:03.967 --> 00:46:06.300
So as part of the process,
we looked at the application.

00:46:06.300 --> 00:46:10.920
We looked at the
existing components.

00:46:10.920 --> 00:46:12.920
We had all the time to do that.

00:46:12.920 --> 00:46:14.460
So I'll emphasize that.

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:16.980
Because there were
elements of re-architecture

00:46:16.980 --> 00:46:19.870
or re-architecting some
components of the application.

00:46:19.870 --> 00:46:22.170
So we had all the time that
we needed to look at it,

00:46:22.170 --> 00:46:24.180
investigate, and
figure out, OK, here

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:26.970
are certain components
of refactoring

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:28.500
that would be required.

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:34.650
Looking at the cost that's
being generated or created,

00:46:34.650 --> 00:46:37.740
we looked at a construct
in Google Cloud Platform

00:46:37.740 --> 00:46:39.510
that's called Preemptible VMs.

00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:42.260
Anybody heard about
Preemptible VMs?

00:46:42.260 --> 00:46:43.360
OK, very few.

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:47.020
Preemptible VMs are among those
key wonderful things in Google

00:46:47.020 --> 00:46:50.740
Cloud where, if your application
is not mission critical,

00:46:50.740 --> 00:46:53.300
you basically procure
a VM and you say,

00:46:53.300 --> 00:46:55.270
I want that to be preemptible.

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:56.320
What does that mean?

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:58.780
Google would schedule
that for you.

00:46:58.780 --> 00:46:59.710
It would run.

00:46:59.710 --> 00:47:03.640
But if Google needs that
capacity for that VM,

00:47:03.640 --> 00:47:06.610
on a one-minute notice,
they'll take it away,

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:08.140
they'll park
everything, and then

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:10.510
when they're done with
that, they'll restart it,

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:13.150
and it will resume.

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:14.620
What do you get in
return for that?

00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:18.070
About 80% discount on
the cost of the VM.

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:19.460
So that's pretty significant.

00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:22.030
So if you have
these workloads that

00:47:22.030 --> 00:47:25.060
do not need to be
on all the time,

00:47:25.060 --> 00:47:28.480
and you can leverage preemptible
VMs, sure, all power to you.

00:47:28.480 --> 00:47:34.180
This client saved a significant
amount of cost, 70% of the cost

00:47:34.180 --> 00:47:37.900
was saved by using
preemptible VMs.

00:47:37.900 --> 00:47:40.750
And we also were
able to re-architect

00:47:40.750 --> 00:47:44.950
some of these applications and
use the microservice pattern

00:47:44.950 --> 00:47:46.730
as part of the process.

00:47:46.730 --> 00:47:49.210
But one of the key lessons
learned for the customer here

00:47:49.210 --> 00:47:52.840
is they did not have enough
people with the right skills

00:47:52.840 --> 00:47:53.380
in DevOps.

00:47:53.380 --> 00:47:56.170
And that's where the
partner came in and provided

00:47:56.170 --> 00:47:58.480
our own methodology
when it comes

00:47:58.480 --> 00:48:02.080
to how you could use
these type of patterns

00:48:02.080 --> 00:48:04.900
within your organization, what
type of assessment that would

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:06.550
be needed, what type
of gaps, and what

00:48:06.550 --> 00:48:10.900
type of recommendations to
fill these gaps were provided.

00:48:10.900 --> 00:48:13.990
And pretty much, it turned out
to be a very successful story

00:48:13.990 --> 00:48:16.750
at the end of the day.

00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:18.887
So the last thing that--

00:48:18.887 --> 00:48:20.470
the question we asked
at the beginning

00:48:20.470 --> 00:48:25.450
of the session, anybody still
thinks that it's a random job?

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:27.120
OK, good, no hands showing.

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:28.370
I'll just leave you with this.

00:48:28.370 --> 00:48:31.250
This is just one comment,
from Dimensional Research,

00:48:31.250 --> 00:48:34.310
why our application
migration products are

00:48:34.310 --> 00:48:36.050
late and over budget.

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:38.740
Basically, the statistics and
the survey that they have done,

00:48:38.740 --> 00:48:41.240
they found that, yes, there is
value in moving to the cloud.

00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:42.948
Lots of companies,
who move to the cloud,

00:48:42.948 --> 00:48:46.880
were able to achieve the
benefit of the cloud.

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:48.594
But the key statement
that they found

00:48:48.594 --> 00:48:50.510
that initial projects
were more difficult than

00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:52.290
expected, taking a year or more.

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:53.630
Some of them are failing.

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:57.950
75% of these
projects took longer.

00:48:57.950 --> 00:49:01.560
50% of the participants revealed
that they are over budget.

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:03.650
So the key things
that were basically

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:06.710
uncovered as a reason for that--
lack of expertise, security

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:10.980
concerns, resource shortages,
and competing priorities

00:49:10.980 --> 00:49:12.620
were listed as the primary ones.

00:49:12.620 --> 00:49:16.550
So it goes back to our
story, don't do over-greed.

00:49:16.550 --> 00:49:18.720
And if you don't have the
right skills, leverage

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:21.680
a partner as part of the
migration, and hopefully

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:22.940
it will be a successful one.

00:49:22.940 --> 00:49:24.020
Thank you.

00:49:24.020 --> 00:49:25.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:26.120 --> 00:49:29.170
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

